      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Rigorous research efforts have been undertaken worldwide to develop a needle-free insulin delivery for many decades with limited success. This translational study aims to deliver insulin through skin with painless electroporation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A recently designed microelectrode array was used to deliver insulin in mice with diabetes under electroporation conditions that are painless and harmless on human skin."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Under such condition, a therapeutic amount of insulin was delivered successfully through mouse skin. Electroporation alone increased insulin transport around 100-fold compared with passive diffusion. Increased skin temperature to 40°C for 20\u2009min augmented insulin transport to 237-fold more than the control value. Repeated electroporation showed no harm on human skin."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The results indicate the potential of painless delivery of insulin through human skin in future clinical practice."
        }
      ]},
